{
  "concept": "A rotation is a constant-time pointer rearrangement that changes a binary search tree's shape — which of x and y is the parent, and who owns the middle subtree β — while preserving the inorder sequence α, x, β, y, γ.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Left configuration: gray circle node labeled y at top with a short vertical stub edge rising to an unnamed parent",
    "Left configuration: gray circle node labeled x as y's left child, connected by a straight edge",
    "Left configuration: edge from x down-left to Greek label α (no circle, bare subtree label)",
    "Left configuration: edge from x down-right to Greek label β",
    "Left configuration: edge from y down-right to Greek label γ",
    "Right configuration: gray circle node labeled x at top with a short vertical stub edge to unnamed parent",
    "Right configuration: edge from x down-left to Greek label α",
    "Right configuration: gray circle node labeled y as x's right child",
    "Right configuration: edge from y down-left to Greek label β",
    "Right configuration: edge from y down-right to Greek label γ",
    "Center upper dotted arrow pointing left, with caption LEFT-ROTATE(T, x) above it in small-caps",
    "Center lower dotted arrow pointing right, with caption RIGHT-ROTATE(T, y) below it in small-caps",
    "Highlight overlay for the pivot edge joining x and y",
    "Highlight overlay for the β subtree link (the only subtree that changes parent)",
    "Optional inorder ribbon beneath each tree listing α, x, β, y, γ left to right",
    "Pointer-assignment trace panel listing the lines executed so far"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "rotateCode",
      "type": "code_editor",
      "teaches": "Rotation is exactly three pointer reassignments plus parent bookkeeping; writing them in the wrong order or forgetting the parent link visibly detaches or orphans a subtree.",
      "label": "Implement LEFT-ROTATE(T, x)",
      "entry_point": "leftRotate(T, x)",
      "api": [
        { "op": "right(n)", "does": "returns the right child of node n (or NIL)" },
        { "op": "left(n)", "does": "returns the left child of node n (or NIL)" },
        { "op": "parent(n)", "does": "returns the parent of node n (or NIL for the root)" },
        { "op": "setLeft(p, c)", "does": "make c the left child of p and set c's parent to p — a mutating op" },
        { "op": "setRight(p, c)", "does": "make c the right child of p and set c's parent to p — a mutating op" },
        { "op": "replaceInParent(old, new)", "does": "attach new where old hung on old's parent, or make new the root if old was the root — a mutating op" },
        { "op": "note(text)", "does": "annotate the current frame with the learner's own comment" }
      ],
      "frame": "the whole 5-item subtree (x, y, α, β, γ plus the parent stub) with the one or two links the op just touched drawn in red and the previous position of a moved subtree ghosted",
      "input": { "label": "Starting subtree (parent stub, x with α, y with β and γ)", "default": "x: left=alpha, right=y; y: left=beta, right=gamma; x has unnamed parent" },
      "sample_code": "function leftRotate(T, x) {\n  const y = right(x);              // y becomes the new subtree root\n  note('y = x.right');\n  setRight(x, left(y));            // beta moves to x's right\n  note('beta reparented to x');\n  replaceInParent(x, y);           // y takes x's place under the parent\n  note('y linked to x.p');\n  setLeft(y, x);                   // x becomes y's left child\n  note('x is now y.left');\n}",
      "buggy_code": "function leftRotate(T, x) {\n  const y = right(x);\n  setLeft(y, x);                   // BUG: attaching x before moving beta\n  setRight(x, left(y));            // left(y) is now x itself — beta is lost and x points at itself\n  replaceInParent(x, y);\n}",
      "success_check": "x is y's left child, α and β are x's children, γ is y's right child, y hangs where x used to hang on the parent, and the inorder walk still reads α, x, β, y, γ"
    },
    {
      "id": "playback",
      "type": "state",
      "teaches": "Each pointer assignment is a discrete, reversible frame; stepping shows exactly when β changes hands and when the parent link flips.",
      "label": "Step through the assignments",
      "states": ["before", "y = x.right", "beta reparented", "parent relinked", "x is y.left", "after"],
      "default": "before"
    },
    {
      "id": "direction",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "LEFT-ROTATE and RIGHT-ROTATE are exact inverses: the same three links, reassigned the other way.",
      "label": "Rotation direction",
      "options": ["LEFT-ROTATE(T, x)", "RIGHT-ROTATE(T, y)"],
      "default": "LEFT-ROTATE(T, x)"
    },
    {
      "id": "inorderRibbon",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "The left-to-right inorder order α, x, β, y, γ is identical before and after, which is why the rotation is a legal BST repair.",
      "label": "Show inorder walk",
      "default": false
    },
    {
      "id": "inspectLink",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "Which pointers a rotation actually touches — only three links change; α and γ never move.",
      "label": "Hover a node or edge"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The two shapes",
      "narration": "The same five items appear in two shapes: on the left y is on top with x as its left child; on the right x is on top with y as its right child. Only the x–y link and the owner of β differ.",
      "state": { "direction": "LEFT-ROTATE(T, x)", "playback": "before", "inorderRibbon": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Order is the invariant",
      "narration": "Turn on the inorder ribbon: both shapes read α, x, β, y, γ from left to right. Rotation is allowed precisely because this sequence is untouched.",
      "state": { "direction": "LEFT-ROTATE(T, x)", "playback": "before", "inorderRibbon": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "β changes parents",
      "narration": "Step to the second assignment. β, which was y's left child, becomes x's right child — this is the only subtree that moves.",
      "state": { "direction": "LEFT-ROTATE(T, x)", "playback": "beta reparented", "inorderRibbon": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "The parent link flips",
      "narration": "Next, y is attached where x used to hang on the unnamed parent. Forgetting this line is the classic bug: the rotated subtree floats free of the tree.",
      "state": { "direction": "LEFT-ROTATE(T, x)", "playback": "parent relinked", "inorderRibbon": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "x descends",
      "narration": "Finally x becomes y's left child. Three assignments, constant time, no subtree ever traversed.",
      "state": { "direction": "LEFT-ROTATE(T, x)", "playback": "after", "inorderRibbon": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Run it backwards",
      "narration": "Switch to RIGHT-ROTATE(T, y) and step again: the identical three links are reassigned in mirror image, returning the original shape.",
      "state": { "direction": "RIGHT-ROTATE(T, y)", "playback": "after", "inorderRibbon": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A rotation moves exactly one subtree — β — and flips one parent-child link, so the tree's shape changes in constant time while the inorder order α, x, β, y, γ stays exactly the same.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two small subtrees side by side on a white background, joined by a central column of two dotted arrows. Nodes are light-gray filled circles with thin black outlines and italic serif letters (x, y); subtree names are bare italic Greek letters (α, β, γ) at the ends of plain edges, with no triangles. Each configuration's top node carries a short vertical stub edge rising off the top to an unnamed parent. Left drawing: y on top, x as left child, α and β under x, γ under y. Right drawing: x on top, α at left, y as right child, β and γ under y. Center: upper dotted arrow pointing left captioned LEFT-ROTATE(T, x) above it; lower dotted arrow pointing right captioned RIGHT-ROTATE(T, y) below it, both in small-caps serif. During execution, color the currently reassigned pointer red, ghost the previous position of β in gray, and tint the active node's outline. The optional inorder ribbon is a thin horizontal strip under each tree reading α x β y γ with tick marks aligning to the node/label x-positions. A small monospace trace panel to the side lists executed assignment lines, dimming lines not yet reached.",
  "_reconstruction_notes": "See reconstructionNotes."
}

